    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral gastroenteritis (A08.4)</content>. Probable viral gastroenteritis after a shared meal, with mild dehydration (BUN 22 mg/dL, creatinine 0.91 mg/dL, sodium 136 mmol/L, potassium 3.6 mmol/L) and no features that call for stool testing or antibiotics. She can drink, so oral rehydration is enough. [S]<list><item>Oral rehydration solution in small, frequent sips, then a normal diet as tolerated.</item><item>Ondansetron 4 mg dissolving tablet every 8 hours as needed for up to two days.</item><item>No loperamide while she has a temperature; no antibiotics.</item><item>Return the same day for blood in the stool, inability to keep fluids down for 12 hours, or fainting.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Focal epilepsy on levetiracetam (G40.109)</content>. A dose vomited within about 15 minutes should be taken again; she missed one evening dose, which carries a small risk of breakthrough seizures. Dehydration and lost sleep lower the seizure threshold. Levetiracetam is cleared by the kidneys, and her kidney function is normal today. [S]<list><item>Take levetiracetam as usual; if a dose is vomited within 15 minutes, take another; take ondansetron 30 minutes before a dose if nauseated.</item><item>If she cannot keep two doses in a row down, come in the same day for intravenous levetiracetam and fluids.</item><item>Seizure precautions and her rescue plan reviewed; she is not driving until she has been well for a week.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults. Krumholz A, et al. Neurology 2015;84(16):1705-1713 (2015); 2017 Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Infectious Diarrhea. Clin Infect Dis 2017;65(12):e45-e80 (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Telephone check tomorrow; six-month review as booked. Next scheduled visit 1849-09-29: six-month review after resection: seizures, mood, and writing. [S]</paragraph></text>

